nnections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership. Weaknesses: regarded as show-off, not open to new ideas, no explicit calmness under pressure, also \"unfriendly\", \"eats unhealthily\" (from original list but not in SMM). So concerns about interpersonal and health.\n\nCandidate B: strengths: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills. Weaknesses: weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks, pretentious, sometimes adopts wrong tone.\n\nCandidate C: strengths: can make correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology, excellent attention skills, puts concern for others above everything. Weaknesses: difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education, regarded as egocentric.\n\nCandidate D: strengths: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously. Weaknesses: regarded as know-it-all, hot temper, moody, loner.\n\nNow we need to decide best candidate for long-distance pilot. Criteria: reliability, technical competence, calmness, decision-making, teamwork/communication, professional development.\n\nLet's assess each:\n\nA: reliability: anticipates danger, leadership. Technical: spatial vision, complex connections. Calmness: unknown. Decision-making: leadership suggests good judgment. Teamwork: show-off, not open to new ideas, unfriendly, may hinder crew. Professional development: not open to new ideas, maybe not develop.\n\nB: reliability: very conscientious, takes responsibility. Technical: good weather assessment, computer skills. Calmness: handles stress well. Decision-making: not explicit but likely good. Teamwork: nasty remarks, pretentious, wrong tone \u2013 negative. Professional development: not mentioned, but maybe okay.\n\nC: reliability: keeps calm, quick correct decisions, concern for others. Technical: understands complicated technology, attention skills. Calmness: keeps calm in crisis. Decision-making: quick correct decisions. Teamwork: difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education \u2013 negative. Professional development: not willing to further education.\n\nD: reliability: takes responsibility, responds to unexpected events, concentration. Technical: solves problems extremely well. Calmness: hot temper suggests possible lack of calmness. Decision-making: problem solving. Teamwork: know-it-all, moody, loner \u2013 negative. Professional development: not mentioned.\n\nThus the strongest candidates in terms of calmness are B (handles stress) and C (keeps calm). B has interpersonal negativity; C has communication issues. Which is more critical? Crew communication is essential; a pilot who cannot communicate effectively may be dangerous. B's nasty remarks also affect crew morale. Both are serious. However B's stress handling is explicitly stated, while C's calmness is also explicit. B's technical skills include weather assessment and computer skills; C's technical includes understanding complicated technology. Both are good.\n\nWe need to weigh which weakness is more tolerable. Possibly a pilot who can be blunt but still works with crew may be manageable, whereas inability to communicate ideas could be more severe. However \"difficulty communicating ideas\" could be mitigated with training, but \"makes nasty remarks\" could cause conflict. Hard.\n\nWe also consider numeric memory weakness for B; could be mitigated with tools, but still important. C's lack of willingness to further education may affect long-term suitability.\n\nGiven the trade-offs, I might still lean towards Candidate B, but I need to acknowledge uncertainties: B's interpersonal behavior could be a dealbreaker; A's calmness unknown; C's communication may be fatal; D's temper is a big risk.\n\nThus I could either keep my vote for A (if I think leadership outweighs unknown calmness) or shift to B. The guidelines say we should consider reasons we might be wrong. Since we have no evidence of A's calmness, that is a major gap. B has explicit stress handling, which is crucial. So I think I should update my vote to Candidate B, acknowledging uncertainty.\n\nThus my public message: I now provisionally favor Candidate B. Summarize key evidence, evidence from others (Agent 1 and 3 favor B, Agent 2 originally favored A). Major alternatives: A, C, D. Main tradeoff: B's interpersonal negativity vs A's unknown calmness and leadership, C's calmness vs communication deficits, D's problem solving vs temper. Remaining uncertainty: B's numeric memory impact, severity of his nasty remarks, A's calmness, C's communication in practice, D's temper control. What would change my vote: evidence that A is calm under pressure, or that B's interpersonal issues cause crew performance problems, or that C's communication is effective, or D can control temper.\n\nConfidence maybe 55%.\n\nNow produce output with METADATA_JSON: {\"agent\": \"agent_2\", \"vote\": \"Candidate B\"}.\n\nProceed."}
